## The fraud desk

The API replays the scored test stream in time order on a simulated clock and pushes each decision to the browser over server sent events. It is a replay worker inside the API process, not a message bus. When the API is asleep, the page plays the same stream from a recorded file and says so on screen.

Every card transaction is scored at the moment it happens, from features computed only from earlier transactions; a test recomputes {{ v('models.fraud_card.leakage_rows') }} sampled rows from scratch and requires the same values. The decline threshold is where the expected cost of false declines and missed fraud is lowest on the validation months, at {{ v('models.fraud_card.cost_false_decline') }} per false decline and each missed fraud's amount plus {{ v('models.fraud_card.cost_handling') }}. Accuracy would have chosen {{ v('models.fraud_card.accuracy_point') }} and cost {{ v('models.fraud_card.cost_acc') }}.

Fraud labels arrive when chargebacks do. On the as of date, {{ v('models.fraud_card.unlabelled_share') }} of the test stream's decisions were still inside the chargeback window, so the desk reports precision only over decisions old enough to be labelled and shows how many it cannot yet judge. A precision figure that ignores that window is not a number a fraud operations manager can use.

The model's AUROC of {{ v('models.fraud_card.test.auroc') }} is a property of the Sparkov generator, whose fraud barely overlaps genuine spending; the validation report says so beside the figure. What carries over to a real desk is the machinery around the score.

